## HOW A FROZEN TEXT IS READ
Every text stands in a fenced block whose first line is `[[TEXT <id>]]`; the text is every line after the id line up to the closing fence, joined by line feeds, no line feed at its end (a new owed item's text is seven lines; a `Closed:` or `Closed in part` text and a history text are one line). Its length and sha256 (UTF-8, unfilled) are in THE FROZEN TABLE; **check every one BEFORE filling; a difference is a STOP.** Fill-ins are `<<NAME>>`, replaced by program only; **no `<<` may remain in anything you write.** `<<WRITE-DATE>>` is `date +%Y-%m-%d` at the write; `<<HHMM>>` the clock of the history write from `date`, once, as `HH:MM`. `<<C-89>>` is P-7's number. `<<BL-OLD8>>` and `<<BL-NEW8>>` are the first 8 hex of `BACKLOG.md`'s sha256 before and after PART 2, from your own prints. `<<CMT-1>>` is J-1's comment id from your write log (its POST or its skip). The owed numbers (85, 93, 94, 106, 126, 133, 134, 143, 146, 148 to 151) and the commit names are frozen in the texts (P-4, P-L). **This kit holds no text alone: a text that fails a check STOPS its step whole** (`STOPPED: NEEDS TUESDAY <text id> <check>`). Dry-fill once, as the drafter did (THE DRY-RUN FIGURES), and read every filled line whole before Step 4: no doubled id, no doubled word at a fill-in. **Count the filled lines as the drafter does (THE DRY-RUN FIGURES); an entry's empty line is an added line, not a filled one.**

